lesson k-magnets v=1 format=teach grade=0 unit=5 subject=physics levels=[easy, medium, hard] {
  title "Magnets"
  goal "Say which things a magnet pulls, count what it picks up, and tell a stronger magnet by what it holds."

  look {
    scene 22x12 {
      magnet m mode=things count=4 sticks=2 show=1 at=canvas(1, 1)
    }
    say """
      A magnet pulls things made of iron and steel, like a nail or a paper clip. The arrows show the
      two things it pulls. It does not pull wood, plastic, glass, or even every metal: a coin stays
      where it is.
      """
  }
  do {
    level easy {
      worked k.magnet-or-not k=0 level=medium
    }
    practice k.magnet-or-not count=2 seed=2
    practice k.magnet-picks count=2 seed=3
    practice k.how-many-stuck count=2 seed=4 easy-count=1
    practice k.clips-chain count=2 seed=5
  }
  try stars=2 {
    show k.not-all-metal
  }
  try stars=3 {
    show k.two-magnets-clips a=6 b=3 easy-a=7 easy-b=2 hard-a=5 hard-b=4
  }
  remember {
    say "A magnet pulls iron and steel, and nothing else."
  }

  grown-ups """
    Read each question aloud. A fridge magnet and a tray of things from a drawer do more than any
    page: let the child guess, then test. The wrong idea to watch is that every metal sticks; try a
    coin and some foil. Keep small magnets away from little children's mouths. Off the page, go on
    a magnet hunt round the kitchen and sort what sticks from what does not. Japan, 幼稚園教育要領
    (kindergarten guidelines), 環境, 内容 (2): "生活の中で，様々な物に触れ，その性質や仕組みに興味や関心をもつ。"
    (Touch many things in daily life and take an interest in how they behave and work.)
    """
  level easy {
    grown-ups "At this level the steel spoon is worked in the teacher's pen first, the things beside the nail or clip are wood and plastic, and the chains are short."
  }
  level hard {
    grown-ups "At this level the things include glass and a steel key, the coin and the foil sit beside the nail, and the magnets to compare are close."
  }
}
